window.docContents[215].resolve({"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#fluid-axisymmetric-swirl":{"contents":"Axisymmetric flow with swirl is the setting of the computed scenario of\n for three-dimensional Euler blowup. Away\nfrom the axis its equations resemble the planar Boussinesq system, with the\nsquared swirl in the role of the temperature.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics","header":"8.6. Axisymmetric Flow with Swirl","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#fluid-axisymmetric-swirl"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-and-weak-evolution":{"contents":"The simplest example shows the smoothing mechanism. Take the separated\ncandidate  and differentiate in time:\n\n-- Compute the time derivative of the separated heat mode.\n#cas [derive t] (exp (-t) * sin x)\n\n\n#cas [derive t]\nDomain: (t, x) ∈ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar ℝ): (exp (-t) * sin x)\nOutput (scalar ℝ): -(exp(-t)*sin(x))\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe result is exactly , because . The\nmode  decays at the rate its own curvature dictates. Higher-frequency\nmodes  decay like , quadratically faster in the\nfrequency, and that is the spectral mechanism behind smoothing. Extend this to\nan infinite mode expansion and you still owe summability estimates before\nconvergence and term-by-term differentiation are legitimate.\n\nFor example, the initial profile  evolves as\n\n\n\nBoth terms have equal amplitude at . At , their amplitudes\nare  and : the shorter wavelength has become eight times smaller\nthan the longer one. Differentiating each term gives , and\nsubstitution at zero gives the initial profile. This smooth periodic example\nillustrates relative decay of frequencies; it has infinite  norm on\nthe whole line.\n\nWe can see the derivative budget in the same teaching calculation. For a\nnonnegative integer , frequency , and ,\n\n\n\nEach spatial derivative costs a factor ; positive-time decay pays for\nevery fixed number of them. The price  grows near the initial\nslice, where the datum may be rough. Reversing the mode evolution replaces\n by , so arbitrarily small errors at high frequencies\ncan become large. This calculation explains smoothing and backward\ninstability for a single mode.\n\nThe polynomial examples from Foundations give another way to see the balance.\nFor the planar polynomial heat solution,\nthe two spatial squares contribute a constant Laplacian, which is matched by\na linear time term. The verified calculation uses the same derivatives as the\nharmonic-polynomial check, but now the spatial terms add instead of canceling.\nThis growing polynomial also explains why decay and finite-energy conclusions\nneed assumptions on the data and domain beyond a vanishing heat residual.\n\nParabolic equations generalize the picture to an elliptic spatial operator\ncoupled to forward evolution. For weak solutions, the time derivative may\nbelong to a dual space; the initial value is then supplied by a continuous\npivot-space representative.\n\nFor the variational evolution framework we follow Chapter 7 of\n, and for the Gelfand-triple formulation and the\ncontinuous-representative theorem, ; the\ncompactness step behind the nonlinear limits follows\n. The classical kernel material follows Evans's\n§2.3. The finite-time singularity mechanisms come from a literature specific to\neach model equation: Kaplan's eigenfunction method and concavity arguments for\nnonlinear heat, moment arguments for Keller–Segel.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations","header":"6.1. Diffusion and weak evolution","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-and-weak-evolution"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points":{"contents":"To find a harmonic function with prescribed boundary values, Dirichlet's\nprinciple suggests minimizing its gradient energy. We can choose admissible\nfunctions with energies approaching the infimum, but we still need a limiting\nfunction that attains it. Compactness supplies a candidate; closedness keeps\nits boundary constraint; lower semicontinuity compares its energy with the\nenergies of the approximating functions.\n\nThis argument applies beyond harmonic functions. The\ndirect-method existence theorem\nproduces a minimizer for every coercive weakly lower semicontinuous\nfunctional on a Hilbert space: coercivity keeps a minimizing sequence\nbounded, reflexivity extracts a weak limit, and lower semicontinuity compares\nthe energy of the limit with the limiting energies. Alongside it, the\nfundamental lemma of the calculus of variations\nturns a vanishing first variation into an equation that holds almost everywhere.\n\nEvans's second edition\n is the main reference:\nChapter 4 for separation, transforms, similarity, and asymptotics, Chapter 8\nfor minimization and first and second variations, and Chapter 9 for nonlinear\nfunctional methods. Morrey's Multiple Integrals in the Calculus of\nVariations and Brezis's functional-analysis treatment supply broader context\nfor integral functionals, weak compactness, and monotone operators.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"9. Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points"}});